 /**
 * @page ShieldLayout Shield Layout
 *
 * @brief Physical layout of the prototype shield.
 * 
 * @verbatim
   +-----------------------------+
   | [gr-1] [gr-2] [gr-2] [gr-2] | <- 4 grove connectors
   |                             |
   |   +--------+   +--------+   |
   |   |        |   |        |   |
   |   |   P1   |   |   P2   |   |  <- 2 potentiometers, P1 Battery Voltage, P2 Level Meter
   |   |        |   |        |   |
   |   +--------+   +--------+   |
   |                             |
   |     +----+       +----+     |
   |     |Red |       |Blck|     |  <- 2 push button switches, Red Flow Meter
   |     +----+       +----+     |
   |                             |
   | [led1] [led3] [led2] [led4] |  <- 4 LEDs, led1 feedback on Red push button, led3 on P1, led4 on P2
   |                             |
   | [Dallas]                    |  <- Dallas one-wire temperature sensor
   +-\                 /---------+
      \---------------/
 * @endverbatim
 *
 *
 * @section SensorSelection Sensor Selection Justification
 *
 * The LoRaWAN node is designed for a rainwater buffering application
 * intended for private households. Rain barrels are commonly used to
 * collect and store rainwater for non-potable purposes such as garden
 * irrigation, reducing the consumption of treated drinking water and
 * mitigating stormwater runoff.
 *
 * The selected sensors support the key monitoring requirements of this
 * use case:
 *
 * - Flow Meter
 *   Measures the amount of water withdrawn from the rain barrel.
 *   This information provides insight into water consumption patterns
 *   and enables users to estimate water savings achieved through the
 *   use of harvested rainwater.
 *
 * - Water Level Sensor
 *   Measures the current fill level of the rain barrel. This is the
 *   primary operational parameter of the system, allowing users to
 *   determine the available water volume and identify periods of low
 *   or high storage capacity.
 *
 * - Battery Voltage Sensor
 *   Monitors the energy source of the LoRaWAN node. Since the device
 *   may be installed outdoors and operate autonomously for extended
 *   periods, battery condition information is essential for reliable
 *   operation and predictive maintenance.
 *
 * During prototype development, the physical sensors are emulated
 * using push buttons and potentiometers on the shield. This approach
 * enables validation of the acquisition software, payload encoding,
 * and LoRaWAN communication without requiring the final sensor
 * hardware.
 *
 *
 * @see ApplicationArchitecture.
 */